#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "9efd33d6-c1d7-55ee-947e-0d067461d5c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-215" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = -6t-3t^2$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6-6t \\\\  & =-6(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087386 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/id #uuid "136e4bd1-f9c4-50a0-9d16-54709f0d6d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-216" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t) = 1+2t+3t^2+4t^3$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t=0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t+3{t}^{2}+4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2+6t+12{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+6t+12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6+24t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=0$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =6+24(0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=0$ is $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e7851bb-80bf-541c-b821-f1c5dd419b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7851bb-80bf-541c-b821-f1c5dd419b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277264} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdac6615-f739-5a3d-8a79-8fa482126b15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "565b31b4-2cd1-5b24-8e88-4ac0f4ecf026" :question/math-academy-id "q-336441" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe acceleration is $70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =24t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=70,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70 & =24t-2 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3\\text{ }\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is $70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at $t=3$ seconds."}]}
